# Decorator

Add behavior around an operation by wrapping the object in other objects with the same interface, each adding one
thing, so the behaviors can be combined freely.

## Use it when

- several optional behaviors go around the same operation (buffering, compression, encryption, retry, logging);
- different configurations need different combinations of them;
- more such behaviors are expected, and today each one would add another `if` inside the operation.

## Do not use it when

- there is a single extra behavior that always applies: put it in the operation;
- the behaviors do not share the operation's interface;
- only the input or output format changes once: that may be an Adapter, or plain code.

## Shape

```java
interface DataSink {
    void write(byte[] data);
}

final class FileSink implements DataSink { /* the real operation */ }

final class Compressing implements DataSink {
    private final DataSink next;
    Compressing(DataSink next) { this.next = next; }
    public void write(byte[] data) { next.write(compress(data)); }
}

final class Encrypting implements DataSink { /* same shape: transform, then call next */ }
final class Counting implements DataSink { /* calls next, then counts the bytes */ }

// the assembly decides the combination, once
DataSink sink = settings.archive()
    ? new Counting(new Encrypting(new Compressing(new FileSink(path))))
    : new Counting(new FileSink(path));
```

## Done right when

- each behavior is a wrapper that does one thing and calls the next;
- the real operation knows nothing about the wrappers;
- the combination for each configuration is decided where the objects are assembled;
- adding a behavior means one new wrapper and a change in the assembly only.

## Usual mistakes

- A wrapper reads a configuration flag to decide whether it should act: the combination is back inside the pieces,
  and the assembly no longer says what runs.
- The innermost operation still checks the configuration.
- One of the behaviors stays as an `if` in the operation while the others are wrappers.
- The wrapper changes the interface (adds methods the clients must call): then it is not transparent anymore.

## Neighbors

- **Chain of Responsibility**: a sequence of handlers where one usually takes the request and stops. A chain or a
  list of filters is a fine alternative to Decorator when every piece is separate and the assembly decides which
  pieces are used.
- **Adapter**: changes the interface, keeps the behavior. Decorator keeps the interface, adds behavior.
- **Proxy**: same interface, but controls access to the object instead of adding behavior.
# Chain of Responsibility

Pass a request along a sequence of handlers; each one either handles it, or passes it on, or stops it.

## Use it when

- several handlers could deal with a request, and which one does depends on the request;
- the set of handlers or their order changes per configuration;
- new handlers are expected, and today each one would add a branch to a central method.

## Do not use it when

- there is always exactly one handler: call it;
- every handler must always run around the same operation and add something: that is closer to Decorator;
- the order and the set never change and fit in one short method.

## Shape

```java
interface ClickHandler {
    boolean handle(Click click);           // true when handled; false to let the next one try
}

final class HandlerChain {
    private final List<ClickHandler> handlers;      // the order is decided where the chain is built
    HandlerChain(List<ClickHandler> handlers) { this.handlers = List.copyOf(handlers); }

    void dispatch(Click click) {
        for (ClickHandler handler : handlers) {
            if (handler.handle(click)) return;
        }
    }
}

final class ButtonHandler implements ClickHandler { /* handles clicks inside the button */ }
final class PanelHandler implements ClickHandler { /* handles what the button did not */ }
final class WindowHandler implements ClickHandler { /* the last resort */ }
```

A linked form (each handler holds `next`) is the same pattern.

## Done right when

- each handler is its own piece and decides only about its own case;
- the list (or links) is built in one place, where the configuration is known;
- the dispatcher knows no handler by name;
- adding a handler means one new class and one change where the chain is built.

## Usual mistakes

- Handlers check a global setting to decide whether they are part of the chain, instead of the builder leaving them
  out.
- The dispatcher still has special cases for some requests before the chain.
- A handler depends on a flag set by a previous one in a hidden way.

## Neighbors

- **Decorator**: every wrapper runs and adds behavior; in a chain, usually one handler takes the request.
- **Strategy**: one rule chosen up front; a chain tries handlers in order.
# State

Represent each state of an object as its own class, so the object behaves differently as its state changes, without
`switch` statements on the state in every method.

## Use it when

- one object moves through states over time, and several of its operations behave differently in each state;
- the transitions themselves carry rules (what is allowed from where, what happens on entering a state);
- adding a state today would mean editing many methods that switch on the current state.

## Do not use it when

- the "state" is just a status field that is read, with one or two simple checks: an enum and a guard clause are
  clearer;
- the allowed transitions fit in a small table (from, event, to) and the behavior does not differ per state;
- the variant is chosen once and never changes: that is Strategy.

## Shape

```java
interface MachineState {
    MachineState insertCoin(VendingMachine m, int cents);
    MachineState selectItem(VendingMachine m, String code);
}

final class Idle implements MachineState { /* insertCoin -> HasCredit; selectItem -> refuse */ }
final class HasCredit implements MachineState { /* selectItem -> Dispensing or refuse */ }
final class Dispensing implements MachineState { /* everything refused until done */ }

final class VendingMachine {
    private MachineState state = new Idle();
    void insertCoin(int cents) { state = state.insertCoin(this, cents); }
    void selectItem(String code) { state = state.selectItem(this, code); }
}
```

## Done right when

- each state class decides what its operations do and which state comes next;
- the context object only delegates to its current state;
- adding a state means one new class and the transitions that lead to it.

## Usual mistakes

- State classes exist, but the context still checks `if (state instanceof …)` before delegating.
- Most methods in most states only throw "not allowed": a transition table would have been simpler.
- Using State for something that never changes during the object's life.

## Neighbors

- **Strategy**: chosen from outside, stays fixed. State is changed by the object itself.
- **A transition table**: enough when states differ only in which transitions are allowed.
# Strategy

Put each variant of a rule in its own object behind one interface, so the code that uses the rule never names the
variants.

## Use it when

- several variants compute or decide the same thing in different ways (a route, a score, a compression, a sort order);
- the variant is chosen per use (by a field, a request, a setting) and does not change on its own afterwards;
- new variants are expected, and today each one would add a branch in more than one place.

## Do not use it when

- the variants differ only in data (a rate, a limit): keep the data in a table or an enum field;
- there are two variants and nothing suggests a third;
- the variants share a fixed sequence of steps and differ inside it: that is Template Method.

## Shape

```java
interface RoutePlanner {
    Route plan(Point from, Point to, MapData map);   // everything a variant needs comes in here
}

final class FastestRoute implements RoutePlanner { public Route plan(Point a, Point b, MapData m) { /* … */ } }
final class ShortestRoute implements RoutePlanner { public Route plan(Point a, Point b, MapData m) { /* … */ } }
final class ScenicRoute implements RoutePlanner { public Route plan(Point a, Point b, MapData m) { /* … */ } }

final class RoutePlanners {                          // the one place that knows the variants
    private final Map<String, RoutePlanner> byName = Map.of(
        "fastest", new FastestRoute(), "shortest", new ShortestRoute(), "scenic", new ScenicRoute());
    RoutePlanner named(String name) { /* look up, reject unknown names */ }
}

// the client only talks to the interface
Route route = planners.named(request.mode()).plan(request.from(), request.to(), map);
```

An `enum` whose constants each carry their own implementation (an abstract method overridden per constant, or a
lambda passed to the constructor) is the same pattern. A `switch` that only picks the implementation is fine; a
`switch` that holds the behavior is not.

## Done right when

- every variant, including the ones that existed before the new ones, is an implementation of the interface;
- the rule of each variant (its calculation, its validation, its exceptions) lives inside that variant;
- the client calls the interface and never checks which variant it holds;
- adding a variant means one new class and one registration.

## Usual mistakes

- Only the new variants become classes, and the old ones stay in a `switch` next to them.
- The interface is too narrow for one variant, so the client special-cases it (`if (planner instanceof ScenicRoute)`)
  to pass extra data. Widen what the variants receive instead.
- Each variant delegates back to a shared helper that still switches on the variant.
- An enum gains methods that `switch (this)`: the branching moved, it did not go away.

## Neighbors

- **State**: the object changes its own variant over time. Strategy is chosen from outside and stays.
- **Template Method**: the variants share a skeleton and differ in some steps.
- **Command**: packages a request to run later; Strategy packages a rule to run now.
